overview.body:
The programmer is a scratch area: its values sit on top of the show (highest priority). They are not part of the project, not saved, and not in the project undo history.
• Select fixtures → set intensity, color, attributes → select another batch and continue;
• With “Live output” on, the fixtures follow the programmer immediately; otherwise you only see it in the preview;
• The programmer has its own undo / redo (arrows at the top), so an accidental “Clear” can be undone;
• “Remove selected” clears only the selected fixtures; “Clear programmer” clears everything.

highlight.body:
Highlight is not written into the programmer; turning it off restores everything.
• It always shows in the preview; real fixtures light up only while “Live output” is on;
• It follows the selection: Alt+← / Alt+→ moves the highlight to the previous / next fixture;
• Channels with a highlight value (OFL highlightValue) use that value.

select.body:
The selection is shared with the preview and the patch list.
• Tools work on the current selection, or on all fixtures when nothing is selected;
• “Every N, take M” is handy for chases and interleaving;
• Fans follow the selection order: fixtures selected first come first.

intensity.body:
Intensity merges with the show by HTP (highest wins): dimming in the programmer does not override a brighter clip.
• +10% / −10% add to each fixture's current value, keeping their differences;
• With one fixture selected, “Locate” flashes it so you can find it on stage;
• “Remove intensity” clears only the intensity layer.

color.body:
Color merges by LTP (latest wins): the programmer color always wins over the show.
• Only the modes the selected fixtures support are shown: color, color temperature, per emitter;
• Each mode remembers its last value, so switching modes doesn't lose your color;
• Fixtures that get a color but no intensity are set to 100% automatically.

attrs.body:
Attributes are grouped into position, beam, focus and control.
• Faders write 0–100%; channels with capabilities (gobo, color wheel, prism…) offer a slot list;
• Values go only to fixtures that have the attribute; partial support shows a count;
• “Mixed” means the selected fixtures differ; moving the fader unifies them;
• × removes the attribute layer.

fan.body:
Each fixture gets A + (B − A) × weight; the mode defines the weights:
• /: symmetric around the middle — the middle fixture is A, the last one is B;
• <: from the first fixture — the first is B, returning to A;
• >: from the last fixture — the first is A, the last is B;
• ><: largest in the middle — ends are A, middle is B;
• <>: largest at the ends — middle is A, ends are B.
The transition sets the spacing: linear, sine, slow start, fast start. The bars below preview each fixture's value.

record.body:
Recording happens at the playhead; each record is one undo step.
• Record as scene clip: different values for several fixtures in one clip (a scene track is created if needed);
• Record to own tracks: one look clip per fixture on its own track; occupied positions are skipped;
• Length in ms or beats (when the project has a tempo), with an optional fade-in;
• Update selected clips: “Merge” changes only the layers in the programmer, “Overwrite” replaces the clip's look.

swatches.body:
Each swatch shows the color the fixture actually emits at the playhead (programmer and highlight included).
• Click to select, Ctrl to add, Shift to select a range in screen order, drag on empty space to marquee-select;
• Arrow keys move, Shift+arrows extend, Ctrl+A selects all visible swatches, Esc clears the selection;
• Search by name, model or address (@12 is address 12, 1.12 is universe 1 address 12); filter to selected, programmed or conflicting fixtures;
• Pixel fixtures show segments in the lower half; click a segment to select that pixel;
• Badges: dot = programmer value, red frame = address conflict, hatch = mirror address, ⚡ = strobing, P = parked channel, crosshair = locating;
• The context menu reveals in patch, locates, clears programmer values and creates tracks; dragging swatches onto the timeline creates tracks too.

timeline.body:
Cues can be dragged, trimmed and split; everything is undoable.
• Time range: Shift + drag on the ruler to select a span; right-click to delete it (closing the gap), insert a gap or copy. With no cue selected, Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X / Delete act on the range and Z zooms to it;
• Ripple edit: when on, deleting or dragging a cue moves later cues on the same track; hold Alt while dragging to affect all tracks plus markers, sections, the loop and media;
• Arrange: select several cues and right-click Arrange to align starts, chain, space by beat, quantize or repeat;
• Tracks: drag the grip on the track header to reorder, drag the bottom edge to resize (double-click resets);
• Keyframes: drag a keyframe on a cue to retime it, hold Ctrl to copy;
• Click the playhead timecode in the toolbar to type a time to jump to (e.g. 1:23.10, +2s, 12f);
• The colour tag filter dims other cues and can select every cue with that tag.
